{
  "id": "quiz-laws-of-indices",
  "title": "Quiz: Laws of Indices",
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Simplify $x^5 \\times x^3$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$x^8$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$2x^8$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$x^2$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$x^{15}$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "a",
      "explanation": "When multiplying powers with the same base, add the indices: $x^5 \\times x^3 = x^{5+3} = x^8$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Simplify $\\frac{y^{10}}{y^4}$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$y^{2.5}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$y^{40}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$y^6$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$y^{14}$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "When dividing powers with the same base, subtract the indices: $\\frac{y^{10}}{y^4} = y^{10-4} = y^6$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Simplify $(a^3)^4$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$a^{34}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$a^{12}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$a^7$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$4a^3$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "b",
      "explanation": "When raising a power to another power, multiply the indices: $(a^3)^4 = a^{3 \\times 4} = a^{12}$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "What is $(3x)^2$?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$9x$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$6x^2$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$3x^2$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$9x^2$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "d",
      "explanation": "$(3x)^2 = 3^2 \\times x^2 = 9x^2$. The power applies to both the 3 and the $x$. A common mistake is writing $3x^2$ which only squares the $x$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Simplify $\\frac{x^8 \\times x^3}{x^4}$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$x^3$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$x^{15}$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$x^6$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$x^7$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "d",
      "explanation": "First combine the numerator: $x^8 \\times x^3 = x^{11}$. Then divide: $\\frac{x^{11}}{x^4} = x^{11-4} = x^7$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Why can you NOT simplify $2^3 \\times 3^2$ by adding the indices?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "Because one index is even and the other is odd"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "Because the numbers are too large"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "Because the bases are different"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "Because the indices are different"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "The index laws (adding indices for multiplication) only apply when the bases are the same. Since 2 and 3 are different bases, you must evaluate each power separately: $2^3 \\times 3^2 = 8 \\times 9 = 72$."
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "type": "multiple-choice",
      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "Simplify $(2a^3b)^2 \\times (3ab^2)$.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$12a^6b^4$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$12a^7b^4$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$6a^4b^3$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$6a^7b^4$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "b",
      "explanation": "$(2a^3b)^2 = 4a^6b^2$. Then $4a^6b^2 \\times 3ab^2 = 12 \\times a^{6+1} \\times b^{2+2} = 12a^7b^4$."
    }
  ]
}
